Since nobody ever dies, and since everyone has at most two children, and since the gender ratio is 1:1, every generation will have exactly 1000 births (since each child has two parents). \textcolor{red}{[Incorrect: The two-child law applies per person, not per couple. If each person can have up to two children, the total number of births per generation could be up to 2000, not 1000, unless there is a restriction per couple.]} Therefore, initial growth is 1000 births every 30 years, which is a growth rate of $1000 / 30 \approx 33.33$ births per year. \textcolor{red}{[This calculation is based on the previous incorrect assumption.]} After a billion years, the population will be roughly $1000 \times 10^9 / 30 \approx 3.33 \times 10^{10}$ inhabitants. \textcolor{red}{[This is a linear growth model, but with no deaths and more than replacement fertility, the population would grow exponentially, not linearly.]} This is clearly more than $10^{10}$, so a Malthusian catastrophe will occur. The first such catastrophe will occur after $10^{10} / 33.33 \approx 300$ million years. \textcolor{red}{[This is based on the incorrect linear growth model.]} It will reduce the population to about $10^9$ people, the vast majority of whom will already have had two children; assuming deaths in the catastrophic state are random, the first Malthusian catastrophe reduces future births to $\approx 3.33$ per year, because it reduces the remaining legally breeding population by a factor of 10. \textcolor{yellow}{[Minor: The logic here is unclear—if the population is reduced by a factor of 10, the number of people able to have children is also reduced, but the birth rate would depend on the age structure and how many people have not yet had children.]} A 700-million year climb follows which takes the final population to $\approx 10^9 + 3.33 \cdot 7 \cdot 10^8 \approx 2.33 \cdot 10^9$. \textcolor{red}{[Again, this is based on the incorrect linear model.]} In the long term, catastrophe states repeat at ever-increasing intervals, each reduces the breeding population by 90 percent, and the population finally hits a steady state at roughly one billion inhabitants. \textcolor{green}{[Good intuition that the population will eventually stabilize near the catastrophe threshold, but the details of the growth and crash cycles need to be based on the correct exponential model.]}

---

**Summary of main issues:**
- \textcolor{red}{The main error is treating population growth as linear when, with no deaths and more than replacement fertility, it would be exponential.}
- \textcolor{red}{The calculation of births per generation is incorrect due to misunderstanding the two-child law.}
- \textcolor{yellow}{Some minor confusion about the effect of catastrophes on future birth rates.}
- \textcolor{green}{Good recognition of the long-term stabilization mechanism.}

**Suggestion:** \textcolor{green}{Review the difference between linear and exponential growth, and clarify the interpretation of the two-child law (per person vs. per couple). Try recalculating the population trajectory using the exponential growth model.}

